  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 306 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nThursday, a mid-level trough axis will move into the Hudson Bay \nregion while a strong 597-dam 500-mb ridge remains anchored across \nthe southwestern United States. The position of the ridge will \nmaintain stronger northwest flow aloft across the region. A weak \ndisturbance is forecast to move along the northern periphery of the \nridge, providing sufficient lift for shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment Thursday afternoon into the evening, especially across \nwestern Nebraska. In terms of severe weather, ample instability \ncombined with stronger deep-layer shear should support organized \nsevere thunderstorms capable of producing large to very large hail \nand damaging winds. This threat is currently highlighted by the SPC \nDay 3 Marginal Risk, and trends will continue to be monitored over \nthe next several forecast cycles. Temperatures Thursday will remain \nin the 80s area-wide, with gusty southerly winds expected across \nmuch of the region.\n\nFriday, the upper-level ridge across the southwestern United States \nwill begin shifting eastward, resulting in increasing warm air \nadvection through the day. Forecast 850-mb temperatures are expected \nto return to the 25 to 30C range, supporting highs from the middle \n80s across eastern portions of the area to the lower 90s across the \nwestern zones. The stronger upper-level flow and better forcing are \nexpected to shift north of the region into the Dakotas, where the \ngreatest coverage of thunderstorms is currently favored. However, \nsome of this convection may propagate southward into north-central \nNebraska Friday night, providing another opportunity for showers and \nthunderstorms. Some severe weather potential may accompany this \nactivity across north-central Nebraska, though confidence in the \ncoverage and magnitude of the threat remains low at this time.\n\nSaturday through early next week, the upper-level ridge is forecast \nto flatten, allowing stronger west to southwest flow aloft to become \nestablished across the region. Saturday currently appears to be the \nwarmest day of the period, with highs reaching the lower to middle \n90s across much of the area. As the ridge flattens and stronger flow \naloft returns, temperatures should moderate back into the 80s \nthrough early next week. With stronger flow aloft overlapping ample \ninstability, the overall pattern will remain supportive of periodic \nsevere weather chances through at least early next week. Saturday \ncurrently appears to have the greatest severe weather potential, \nparticularly across western and central Nebraska. In terms of \nprecipitation, widespread meaningful rainfall is not expected \nthrough the extended period. However, precipitable water values will \nremain elevated, supporting efficient rainfall processes and locally \nheavy rainfall with any thunderstorms that develop. Therefore, \nrainfall amounts will likely remain highly variable, with locally \nhigher amounts possible where thunderstorms occur.",
